+# S3-H2-Cursor hand-back
+
+S3-H2-Cursor to S3-Harness2, 2026-10-22. PR: https://github.com/nobul-tech/aitools/pull/110 (stacked on #100; merge after #100). Branch: `feature/harness-cursor-user-rules`.
+
+## Summary
+
+1. `setup-user-cursor.sh/.ps1` step 4 reads `~/.aitools/rules/.aitools-rules-manifest`. It fans out only names whose source is `shared` or `dotprofile` (case-insensitive), and skips `*.bak.*` and the manifest silently.
+2. For each `aitools` or `retired` name, it backs up and removes `~/.cursor/rules/<stem>.mdc`, but only when the fan-out created it. Recognition:
+   - the new aitools-fanout marker, which is the line after the frontmatter: `<!-- aitools-fanout: generated by setup-user-cursor from ~/.aitools/rules/<name>; edit that rule, not this file -->`;
+   - or, for #87-era files without a marker, content equal to the conversion of the harness copy or of one of its `*.bak.*` backups.
+   Any other file of that name is kept, with a WARN and an ACTION row listing it, because Cursor still loads it.
+3. With no manifest or a bad header, nothing is fanned out and nothing is removed. The step writes a WARN row (`no rules manifest` / `rules manifest not v1`) and the ACTION "Run aitools to rebuild ~/.aitools/rules and its manifest". Invalid lines are ignored with a warning.
+4. Tests: 164 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped (dupcase needs a case-sensitive file system). A new `step4-driver.ps1` runs the `.ps1` step 4 on pwsh/macOS, and every manifest case compares its tree and summary rows with the `.sh` run.
+5. Integration (isolated HOME, real worktree sources): deploy-harness, then a #87-era `~/.cursor/rules` of 29 files. Result: 26 aitools rules removed, 3 user-level rules kept. The MDM build (fixture dotprofile) gives the same result. **No build-deploy.sh change is needed.**
+
+## build-deploy.sh (for S3-H2-MDM)
+
+No change is needed. `build-deploy.sh` extracts the header span `^# Does [a-z]+ things:` .. `^# --- BEGIN cursor body` (scripts/build-deploy.sh:860-861 and :900-901) and the body `BEGIN/END cursor body` whole (:836-837 and :877-878). All new code sits inside those spans.
+
+Verified as follows:
+- Ran `AITOOLS_USER_REPO_PATH=<fixture> HOME=<isolated> bash scripts/build-deploy.sh`.
+- `grep -c 'Step 4: Cursor user rules'` is 1 in each generated script.
+- The `.sh` has 2 `aitools-rules-manifest` references and the `.ps1` has 2 `Read-RulesManifest` references.
+- `bash -n` passes on the generated `.sh`.
+- The generated `.sh` run on the fixture gave 26 removed and 3 kept.
+
+The MDM sequence must still run deploy-harness first, so that the manifest exists. Otherwise the step reports WARN `no rules manifest` and changes nothing.
+
+## Verification
+
+Commands and results:
+- `bash -n` on `scripts/setup-user-cursor.sh` and the test script; the pwsh parser on `setup-user-cursor.ps1`, `step4-driver.ps1` and `convert-rule.ps1`: all clean.
+- `bash tests/rules-fanout-cursor/test-rules-fanout-cursor.sh`: 164 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped (it was 145 before the review fixes).
+- `.scratch/session-h2cursor/integ.sh` (scratch, not tracked):
+  - Manifest: 26 aitools, 2 shared, 1 dotprofile.
+  - First run: `removed: 26`, `WARN|cursor rules|3 updated` (marker added; non-interactive overwrite warns).
+  - Rerun: `OK|cursor rules|3 verified`.
+- `.scratch/session-h2cursor/mdm-check.sh`: the same result from the generated `deploy/setup-user-cursor.sh`.
+- `check-pre-commit.sh`: only the known step 6 FAIL. `check-pre-push.sh`: only the known step 7 FAIL.
+
+## Not run
+
+- The `.ps1` on Windows. On macOS it ran only through the driver.
+- Cursor loading a marker-bearing `.mdc`, because the CLI is not authenticated. The marker is body text after the frontmatter.
+- An interactive run. The first run after merge shows one diff per user-level rule (the marker line).
+- dupcase on Linux.
+
+## Decisions taken
+
+- **Recognition** is the marker plus a legacy content match. The match is cited in `cursor_rule_is_fanout` / `Test-CursorFanoutRule`, and in the prune block of both scripts.
+- **Limitation:** a #87-era `.mdc` whose matching harness copy and backups are all gone (backup_file keeps 20) cannot be recognised. It is kept, with a WARN and an ACTION row.
+- **Dev-path wiring:** the reviewer noted that on this branch `scripts/aitools` and `aitools-install.sh` do not run deploy-harness. PR #106 (S3-H2-Hookin) wires it in. Until #106 merges, a dev run reports WARN `no rules manifest` and changes nothing, which is the safe behaviour.
+- **Safe behaviour without a manifest:** do nothing, with WARN and ACTION. This is what the launch prompt suggested.
+- **Missing user-level source file:** a warning, not an error.
+- **Summary row:** when rules were fanned out, removals appear as `DETAIL|cursor rules|removed: <f>` rows plus the log count. The tracker has no `removed` outcome, and aitools-lib is out of scope.
+- **PS1 backups:** a name with `[ ] * ?` is backed up with `Copy-Item -LiteralPath` instead of Backup-File.
+
+## The #100 follow-up claim
+
+"setup-user-cursor.ps1 logs Ignoring non-rule file ... .aitools-rules-manifest" does not reproduce. On origin/main `scripts/setup-user-cursor.ps1:493`, `if ($entry.Name.StartsWith(".")) { continue }` runs before the log at :497. A new test asserts that neither script mentions the manifest.
+
+## Protected-file drafts awaiting approval
+
+- `S3-H2-Cursor-drafts/reference/tool-ops-cursor.md.edit.txt` (the target file comes from open PR #99)
+- `S3-H2-Cursor-drafts/registries/tool-ops.json.edit.txt` (`tools.cursor.deployedBy`, from PR #99)
+
+## WRITE_BLOCKED
+
+None.
+
+## INCIDENT markers (not filed)
+
+1. INCIDENT: `Backup-File` in `scripts/aitools-lib.ps1:781-805` uses `Test-Path $FilePath` and `Copy-Item -Path`, both of which treat the path as a wildcard. A name containing `[ ]` gets no backup, yet `$script:BackupFileOk` stays `$true`, so callers (`Deploy-ManagedFile`, setup-user-cursor before this PR) write or remove without a backup. Fix: switch to `-LiteralPath` in the library.
